1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,240
Generics taught the compiler what a
collection holds. Sometimes what we need
to say is not a type parameter, but a note
about intent — a note that another tool
should notice.

2
00:00:10,359 --> 00:00:23,039
Suppose you override toString on a domain
class. You type carefully… and misspell
the method as tostring. Without help, Java
treats that as a brand-new method. Your
override never runs.

3
00:00:23,159 --> 00:00:35,959
Logging still shows the useless default.
The compiler did not fail you; it simply
never knew you meant to override. So the
question appears: can we declare intent so
the compiler verifies it?

4
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:43,100
Annotations are structured metadata
attached to declarations. Compilers, build
tools, and frameworks listen to them.

5
00:00:43,219 --> 00:00:55,939
Think of an annotation as a structured
sticky note: humans can read it, but more
importantly, programs can read it without
parsing comments. Look at this java for a
moment. @Override is the smallest useful
illustration.

6
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:07,260
It does not change runtime behavior by
itself. It tells the compiler: this method
must match a superclass or interface
method. If the signature is wrong,
compilation fails.

7
00:01:07,379 --> 00:01:19,819
Put @Override on every method you believe
is an override. Let the compiler argue
with you early. Once you accept that
metadata can drive tools, retention
becomes the next natural question.

8
00:01:19,939 --> 00:01:28,700
How long does the annotation live? Java
defines three retentions. SOURCE means the
annotation helps during compilation and
may disappear afterward.

9
00:01:28,819 --> 00:01:41,859
CLASS means it is recorded in the class
file but not necessarily visible through
normal reflection. RUNTIME means the
annotation remains available while the
program runs, so frameworks can read it
with reflection.

10
00:01:41,979 --> 00:01:54,379
Assuming all annotations exist at runtime
is a common misunderstanding; check
@Retention before you blame the framework.
That runtime case is why annotations feel
ubiquitous in modern Java.

11
00:01:54,499 --> 00:02:00,859
Dependency injection, web mappings, test
runners, and serializers lean on runtime
annotations.

12
00:02:00,979 --> 00:02:13,780
A method marked for a request path, a
field marked for injection, a test marked
to ignore — the framework scans, reads the
metadata, and wires behavior. Look at this
java for a moment.

13
00:02:13,899 --> 00:02:23,179
A custom annotation like @Route does
nothing alone. Custom annotations need
processors at build time, or reflection at
runtime, to mean anything.

14
00:02:23,299 --> 00:02:31,299
Writing @Route without a reader is like
writing a sticky note and never looking at
it. What if we over-annotate instead of
simplifying design?

15
00:02:31,479 --> 00:02:40,959
Teams sometimes decorate every class with
a forest of markers while the underlying
API stays confused. Annotations do not
replace good API design.

16
00:02:41,079 --> 00:02:50,719
They amplify a design that already makes
sense, and they can obscure one that does
not. Prefer a clear method and a clear
type. Then add the annotation that tools
need.

17
00:02:50,839 --> 00:03:01,159
Now watch how a test runner might use the
same idea: Look at this java for a moment.
A tiny runner would scan MathTest, keep
methods annotated with @Test, and invoke
them.

18
00:03:01,279 --> 00:03:11,679
Without @Retention(RUNTIME), reflective
lookup finds nothing and the suite looks
empty for mysterious reasons. The
annotation is a signal — the design still
has to be worth signaling.

19
00:03:11,799 --> 00:03:22,439
A missed override showed why intent needs
verification. @Override answered with
compiler-checked metadata. Retention
explained how long that metadata survives.

20
00:03:22,559 --> 00:03:30,799
Frameworks showed why runtime annotations
dominate application architecture. Custom
annotations revealed they need processors
or reflection.

21
00:03:30,919 --> 00:03:41,479
A final check when you invent a custom
annotation: name the consumer in the same
pull request. If nobody reads it — no
processor, no reflective scanner, no
compiler plugin — delete it.

22
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:53,039
Dead metadata is worse than no metadata
because it teaches the team that
annotations are decorative. And if
frameworks read annotations at runtime by
inspecting classes and methods, how does
that inspection work?

23
00:03:53,219 --> 00:03:58,019
What API lets a program look at itself?
That curiosity is reflection.
